  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How Cyrillic works",
      "text": [
        "Russian is written in the Cyrillic alphabet: 33 letters, most of which stand for one sound each. Some look like Latin letters but sound different — Р is r, Н is n, В is v, С is s, У is u, Х is kh, and Е is ye.",
        "Most consonants come in two versions, hard and soft. A soft consonant is said with the tongue raised toward a y-sound; it's shown in the IPA with a small ʲ (дядя [ˈdʲædʲə]). The vowel letters е, ё, и, ю, я and the soft sign ь make the consonant before them soft."
      ]
    },
